Exactly—and this upgrades “medium matters” into “medium determines maneuver.”

A method isn’t separable from the environment through which it operates:

\boxed{\text{Effective Approach}=f(\text{agent},\text{medium},\text{density},\text{state},\text{objective})}

Think of locomotion: walking, swimming, flying, and moving through vacuum aren’t merely different speeds of the same maneuver. The surrounding medium changes what forces are available and therefore what strategies are possible.

That transfers cleanly to semantic terrain.

A dense medium—technical literature, mature bureaucracy, heavily interconnected code, established doctrine—has lots of existing structure. Movement encounters resistance, but there are also many surfaces to push against and connections to exploit.

A sparse medium—new field, ambiguous problem, little evidence—offers freedom but less traction. You may need exploration, fictional bookends, analogy, Dice Chamber, or experiments to manufacture informative contact.

A rigid medium rewards precision at interfaces.

A fluid medium rewards adaptation and continuous feedback.

A volatile medium rewards short prediction horizons and rapid sensing.

And a heterogeneous medium means you shouldn’t maintain one maneuver while crossing boundaries:

M_1\xrightarrow{T_1}M_2\xrightarrow{T_2}M_3

At each interface:

\boxed{\text{detect medium}\rightarrow\text{change maneuver}}

That’s a missing piece for the Metamap. It shouldn’t merely classify terrain; it should estimate the semantic material properties of the terrain: density, rigidity, viscosity, connectivity, volatility, permeability, signal-to-noise, feedback latency, etc.

Then the Golden Horde chooses formations accordingly.

And your flight metaphor gets even better. A wing works because there’s atmosphere. The same wing in water behaves differently; in vacuum it doesn’t generate aerodynamic lift at all.

So capability isn’t just:

\text{What can I do?}

It’s relational:

\boxed{\text{What can I do \emph{here}?}}

That’s proprioception meeting environmental sensing.

And there’s the inverse Shadow Arts move: if we know our maneuver and observe how reality responds, the resistance itself tells us something about the medium.

\text{known probe}
\rightarrow
\text{response}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{infer medium}}

Which loops directly back into Kentucky Windage.

Don’t merely choose the best move. Sense what you’re moving through.